WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.547
Picture a parcel that comes back to your door, with a sticker on it.

2
00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:08.970
One sticker says, no such street.

3
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.574
Another says, nobody at this apartment.

4
00:00:11.574 --> 00:00:14.445
And a third parcel never comes back at all.

5
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:19.211
Each sticker points to a different mistake, and each needs a different fix.

6
00:00:19.211 --> 00:00:20.969
Computers leave stickers too.

7
00:00:20.969 --> 00:00:22.665
We call them error messages.

8
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:23.864
Here's the trap.

9
00:00:23.864 --> 00:00:26.357
Every failure feels like the same thing.

10
00:00:26.357 --> 00:00:27.417
It can't connect.

11
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:32.216
So people restart things at random, when the sticker already said what broke.

12
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:36.770
Restarting at random is the network version of shaking a vending machine.

13
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.264
Sometimes it works, and you learn nothing.

14
00:00:39.264 --> 00:00:41.819
So let's learn to read the sticker instead.

15
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:44.378
Here's our running case.

16
00:00:44.378 --> 00:00:51.354
hello-devops, the course's tiny web app, keeps its visit count in a small database called Redis.

17
00:00:51.354 --> 00:00:55.133
Redis runs in a container named cache, on port 6379.

18
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:58.353
And a colleague says, the app can't reach cache.

19
00:00:58.353 --> 00:01:03.081
Before we read any error, let's follow one message that works, step by step.

20
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:04.995
Step one, the name.

21
00:01:04.995 --> 00:01:07.789
Machines connect to addresses, not names.

22
00:01:07.789 --> 00:01:11.674
So the app first asks DNS, the phone book of the network.

23
00:01:11.674 --> 00:01:13.583
What's the address of cache?

24
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.093
The answer comes back.

25
00:01:16.093 --> 00:01:17.239
172.20.0.2.

26
00:01:17.239 --> 00:01:21.095
The name has become a street address.

27
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:28.681
Out on the internet, that question can pass through a whole chain of servers, and each one keeps the answer for a while.

28
00:01:28.681 --> 00:01:30.465
That while is called its TTL.

29
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.932
Step two, the address.

30
00:01:32.932 --> 00:01:37.976
The message travels to the machine at 172.20.0.2.

31
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.768
Step three, the door.

32
00:01:39.768 --> 00:01:46.188
One machine runs many programs, so a port picks the program, like an apartment number.

33
00:01:46.188 --> 00:01:48.503
Redis listens behind door 6379.

34
00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:55.946
Before any data moves, TCP, the registered mail of the network, does a three-step handshake.

35
00:01:55.946 --> 00:01:56.246
SYN.

36
00:01:56.246 --> 00:01:57.373
I want to talk.

37
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.410
SYN-ACK.

38
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.131
Fine, I hear you.

39
00:02:00.333 --> 00:02:00.671
ACK.

40
00:02:00.671 --> 00:02:02.949
And the connection is open.

41
00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.702
Name, address, door.

42
00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:05.624
Three steps.

43
00:02:05.624 --> 00:02:08.157
And each one has its own sticker.

44
00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:10.857
Back to our colleague.

45
00:02:10.857 --> 00:02:12.617
The app can't reach cache.

46
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:15.055
The obvious fix is to restart Redis.

47
00:02:15.055 --> 00:02:17.223
Before you do, read the sticker.

48
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:18.627
Here it is.

49
00:02:18.627 --> 00:02:28.284
nc -zv cache 6379 says, getaddrinfo for host cache, port 6379, Name or service not known.

50
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.664
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:29.664 --> 00:02:30.901
Which step broke?

52
00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:33.448
The name, the address, or the door?

53
00:02:35.633 --> 00:02:36.236
The name.

54
00:02:36.236 --> 00:02:43.668
getaddrinfo is the function programs call to look up a name, and it failed before a single packet left the app.

55
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.507
So restarting Redis would change nothing.

56
00:02:46.507 --> 00:02:48.479
Redis never even heard a knock.

57
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.694
That's the no such street sticker.

58
00:02:50.694 --> 00:02:52.688
The house can be in perfect shape.

59
00:02:52.688 --> 00:02:55.035
The postman still can't find the street.

60
00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:57.796
In the lab, the cause was simple.

61
00:02:57.796 --> 00:03:05.229
The client container sat on Docker's default network, so its phone book had never heard of cache.

62
00:03:05.229 --> 00:03:09.751
Connect it to shopnet, and the name resolves to 172.20.0.2.

63
00:03:09.967 --> 00:03:14.091
So fix the step the sticker names, not the one you happen to be looking at.

64
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.958
Now knock on a door where nobody lives.

65
00:03:17.958 --> 00:03:19.647
nc -zv cache 6380.

66
00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:24.152
The machine is there, so it answers the SYN at once, with a reset.

67
00:03:24.152 --> 00:03:25.385
Connection refused.

68
00:03:25.385 --> 00:03:27.268
Nothing listens on that port.

69
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.753
Nobody at this apartment.

70
00:03:29.753 --> 00:03:39.759
The app is down, on another port, or bound to 127.0.0.1, so it only answers knocks from inside its own machine.

71
00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.747
And the parcel that never comes back?

72
00:03:42.747 --> 00:03:43.840
That's a timeout.

73
00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.319
Nothing answers at all.

74
00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:49.951
A firewall dropped the packets, or the address is wrong, or unreachable.

75
00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:51.299
Pause and guess.

76
00:03:51.299 --> 00:03:54.980
Which sticker arrives faster, refused, or timed out?

77
00:03:57.167 --> 00:03:59.947
Refused comes back in about a millisecond.

78
00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:06.690
On a timeout, your machine resends the SYN a few times, waiting longer each time, before it gives up.

79
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.628
So even the waiting is a clue.

80
00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:10.775
One more sticker.

81
00:04:10.775 --> 00:04:13.193
Connection reset, or an empty reply.

82
00:04:13.193 --> 00:04:17.760
Something accepted the parcel at the front desk, then shut the door.

83
00:04:17.967 --> 00:04:25.009
Proxies, and Docker's port forwarder, do this when the app behind them is unreachable.

84
00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:30.332
For example, an app inside a container that's bound to 127.0.0.1.

85
00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:32.413
So here is the rule.

86
00:04:32.413 --> 00:04:34.908
Read the error before you fix anything.

87
00:04:34.908 --> 00:04:36.956
Each one names a different step.

88
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:40.086
Two cautions about the toolbox.

89
00:04:40.086 --> 00:04:44.883
ping uses a small protocol called ICMP, which many firewalls block.

90
00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:47.103
So a failed ping proves little.

91
00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:54.163
And dig asks DNS servers directly, and ignores /etc/hosts, a local file that beats DNS.

92
00:04:54.163 --> 00:04:56.596
Your apps read that file first.

93
00:04:56.596 --> 00:05:01.071
So if dig and your app disagree about a name, look there.

94
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.149
And every one of these tools has the same limit.

95
00:05:04.149 --> 00:05:07.592
They tell you whether a message arrived, not what it said.

96
00:05:07.592 --> 00:05:08.957
That's the next lesson.

97
00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:11.526
So, the moral of the story.

98
00:05:11.526 --> 00:05:14.068
An error isn't a mood, it's a location.

99
00:05:14.068 --> 00:05:16.024
No such street means the name.

100
00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:17.784
Nobody home means the door.

101
00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:20.782
Silence means something dropped it on the way.

102
00:05:20.782 --> 00:05:23.780
And a shut door means the app behind the desk.

103
00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:25.899
A quick check.

104
00:05:25.899 --> 00:05:29.518
nc -zv db.internal 22 succeeds at once.

105
00:05:29.518 --> 00:05:35.736
Port 5432, the database port, waits thirty seconds, then times out.

106
00:05:35.736 --> 00:05:37.314
Which step broke?

107
00:05:37.314 --> 00:05:38.799
Pause and guess.

108
00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:44.734
The name resolved, and the host answered on port 22.

109
00:05:44.734 --> 00:05:47.704
A stopped database would be refused fast.

110
00:05:47.704 --> 00:05:52.123
So the silence points to a firewall dropping traffic to 5432.
